WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:05.320
How do you follow a cell that leaves home?

2
00:00:06.300 --> 00:00:08.140
You have to mark it before it goes.

3
00:00:08.680 --> 00:00:10.100
And the mark has to last.

4
00:00:11.083 --> 00:00:16.663
Julia Platt opened the question in 1893, working on the mudpuppy, a salamander.

5
00:00:17.667 --> 00:00:22.287
In fixed sections, she followed cells from the crest into the arches, where head

6
00:00:22.287 --> 00:00:23.287
cartilage forms.

7
00:00:24.200 --> 00:00:28.280
Cartilage was supposed to be mesoderm, and her evidence was indirect.

8
00:00:29.200 --> 00:00:30.380
The claim was long doubted.

9
00:00:31.400 --> 00:00:33.940
The lasting mark came from Nicole Le Douarin.

10
00:00:34.720 --> 00:00:36.920
Quail nuclei carry a dense block of chromatin.

11
00:00:37.940 --> 00:00:38.960
Chick nuclei lack it.

12
00:00:39.833 --> 00:00:41.553
The mark is built into the cell.

13
00:00:42.413 --> 00:00:44.753
It neither fades nor dilutes as cells divide.

14
00:00:45.817 --> 00:00:48.837
Graft quail neural tube into a chick at the same level.

15
00:00:48.837 --> 00:00:51.177
Then map where the quail cells go.

16
00:00:52.117 --> 00:00:54.397
But a same-level graft cannot say why.

17
00:00:55.517 --> 00:00:57.257
Origin and route are tangled together.

18
00:00:58.137 --> 00:00:59.757
To separate them, move the cells.

19
00:01:00.800 --> 00:01:04.340
Predict. Take trunk crest from the adrenal level.

20
00:01:05.200 --> 00:01:08.460
At home it makes cells that release noradrenaline and adrenaline.

21
00:01:09.440 --> 00:01:10.460
Move it into the neck.

22
00:01:11.340 --> 00:01:13.600
Where will it go, and what transmitter will it use?

23
00:01:19.933 --> 00:01:22.673
They go where neck crest goes, into the gut wall.

24
00:01:23.573 --> 00:01:25.093
There they form enteric ganglia.

25
00:01:26.117 --> 00:01:29.797
Some donor neurons are cholinergic: they use acetylcholine.

26
00:01:30.617 --> 00:01:32.897
The new site changed what the population made.

27
00:01:35.267 --> 00:01:40.447
Swap the other way, and neck crest placed at the adrenal level makes adrenal medulla

28
00:01:40.447 --> 00:01:42.047
and sympathetic derivatives.

29
00:01:43.150 --> 00:01:44.910
The skeleton behaves differently.

30
00:01:45.930 --> 00:01:49.730
In most early grafts, trunk crest in the head made little or no cartilage.

31
00:01:50.783 --> 00:01:53.903
Under some conditions, trunk crest can make cartilage.

32
00:01:54.863 --> 00:01:58.123
So the start biases the options, and the surroundings help choose.

33
00:01:59.183 --> 00:02:03.923
Now predict. One cell in a dish makes neurons and pigment cells.

34
00:02:04.763 --> 00:02:05.923
Would it make both in the embryo?

35
00:02:12.433 --> 00:02:16.193
Not necessarily. A dish shows what a cell can make.

36
00:02:16.193 --> 00:02:19.193
Tracing in the embryo shows what it did make.

37
00:02:20.083 --> 00:02:24.943
In 1988, both methods found some single cells that made several types.

38
00:02:25.823 --> 00:02:27.963
Whether the typical cell does is still debated.

39
00:02:28.767 --> 00:02:32.547
The takeaway. Move the cells, and the pattern appears.

40
00:02:33.467 --> 00:02:36.387
The start biases the options, and the journey helps choose.
